 ## Overview
-Marmitt's Ray-Cell Intersection computes the point where a ray intersects an isosurface defined by trilinear interpolation within a 3D voxel cell [S1, S2]. It solves for the first positive root of the cubic polynomial derived from the cell's corner values using an iterative bisection refinement strategy [S1, S2]. Unlike analytical cubic solvers (e.g., Schwarze’s method), this approach is slightly faster and yields single-precision, numerically stable results [S1, S2]. It builds on [Neubauer's Ray-Voxel Intersection Algorithm](975-neubauer-s-ray-voxel-intersection-algorithm.md) by applying iterative root finding specifically to the reconstructed cell geometry.
+Marmitt's Ray-Cell Intersection computes the intersection point of a ray with an isosurface defined by trilinear interpolation inside a 3D voxel cell [S1, S2]. It solves for the first positive root of the cubic polynomial derived from the cell's corner values using an iterative bisection refinement strategy [S1, S2]. Compared to analytical cubic solvers like Schwarze’s method, this approach is slightly faster and yields single-precision, numerically stable results [S1, S2]. It implements the Neubauer iterative root finder [S1, S2].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- Ray origin $\mathbf{o}$ and direction $\mathbf{d}$ (normalized).
-- Isosurface value $I_{iso}$.
-- Eight scalar values $v_0, \dots, v_7$ at the corners of the 3D cell. The cell is unit-aligned in local coordinates, with corners at $(x_i, y_i, z_i)$ where $x_i, y_i, z_i \in \{0, 1\}$. The standard ordering corresponds to the trilinear interpolation basis functions.
+- Ray origin $\mathbf{o}$ and direction $\mathbf{d}$ [G].
+- Isosurface value $I_{\text{iso}}$ [S1, S2].
+- Eight scalar values $v_0, \dots, v_7$ at the corners of the 3D cell [S1, S2].
 
 **Outputs:**
-- Intersection parameter $t$ along the ray. If no root exists in $[0, \infty)$, the method reports no intersection.
+- Intersection parameter $t$ along the ray [G]. If no root exists in $[0, \infty)$, the method reports no intersection [G].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-- The cell is defined by 8 scalar values. No additional geometric data structures are required beyond the corner values and the ray parameters.
